How to reduce hallucinations systematically:

Ground every claim in source IDs. Claims without IDs are rejected at validation.
Default to NOT PROVIDED. Missing data is an explicit value, never a guess.
Separate extraction from reasoning. The reasoning stage cannot access facts that were not extracted and recorded.
Compute numbers in code. The model never performs arithmetic that determines a deliverable.
Use a separate verifier. A different prompt and, where possible, a different model checks the output.
Build a golden test set. Maintain 50 to 100 labeled cases, including edge cases and adversarial inputs. Run it on every prompt change and track the rate of unsupported claims as a headline metric.
Calibrate confidence. Compare stated confidence against accuracy on the test set, and adjust thresholds for human review.
Monitor in production. Log flagged outputs, injection attempts, and human corrections. Use the corrections to add new test cases.

Why this works as a strategy rather than a prompt: Each stage has one job and a defined output contract, so a failure can be traced to a stage. The technique is chosen per stage based on the task’s needs. Hallucination control comes from the system, through grounding, validation, and measurement, not from a single line in one prompt.